  "steps": [
    {
      "place": "chandor",
      "phase": "vassals",
      "title": "Heirs of Krishna?",
      "native": "सेऊणदेश",
      "when": "c. 860 to 1187 CE",
      "at": 8,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          74.6,
          20.4
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "The Yadavas claimed descent from Krishna’s clan of Dwarka. Their inscriptions name Dridhaprahara, in the 9th century, as founder, at Chandradityapura, thought to be Chandor near Nashik. His son Seunachandra gave the land its name, Seunadesha, so the dynasty is also called the Seunas. For three centuries they served as vassals of the Rashtrakutas, then of the Chalukyas of Kalyani.",
      "today": "Chandor (Chandwad), Nashik district, Maharashtra."
    },
    {
      "place": "devagiri",
      "phase": "kingdom",
      "title": "A fort on a hill",
      "native": "देवगिरी",
      "when": "c. 1187 to 1191 CE",
      "at": 19,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75.9,
          19.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "When the Chalukya kingdom broke up, Bhillama V seized its northern lands and took the title of king, about 1187. He made Devagiri, ‘hill of the gods’, his capital: a cone of rock with sheer sides, later ringed by moats. Pushing south, he met the Hoysala Ballala II and was beaten at Soratur in 1190. They fought for decades.",
      "today": "Daulatabad Fort, near Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar (Aurangabad), Maharashtra."
    },
    {
      "place": "patan",
      "phase": "kingdom",
      "title": "Stars and music",
      "native": "पाटण",
      "when": "1207 to 1246 CE",
      "at": 40,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75,
          20.2
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Singhana II made the Yadavas the leading power of the Deccan, beating the Hoysalas and the Paramaras of Malwa and taking Kolhapur. At Patan a 1207 inscription records a school founded by Changadeva, Singhana’s chief astrologer and grandson of the mathematician Bhaskara II, to teach Bhaskara’s works. At Singhana’s court Sharngadeva wrote the Sangita Ratnakara, a classic of Indian music.",
      "today": "Patne, near Chalisgaon in Jalgaon district, Maharashtra."
    },
    {
      "place": "paithan",
      "phase": "kingdom",
      "title": "Chakradhar and the Mahanubhavas",
      "native": "पैठण",
      "when": "c. 1260s to 1278 CE",
      "at": 62,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75.2,
          19.7
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Chakradhar Swami wandered and taught around Paithan, preaching devotion to Krishna in Marathi and welcoming women and people of every caste. His followers, the Mahanubhavas, wrote down his life: Mhaimbhat’s Lilacharitra, of about 1278, is counted the first prose work in Marathi. They say he left for the north in 1274; another account says he was executed.",
      "today": "Paithan, on the Godavari; the Mahanubhavas still revere the places he visited."
    },
    {
      "place": "devagiri",
      "phase": "kingdom",
      "title": "Hemadri’s encyclopaedia",
      "native": "हेमाद्री",
      "when": "1260s to c. 1300 CE",
      "at": 68,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75.9,
          19.9
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Hemadri (Hemadpant) served the kings Mahadeva and Ramachandra as chief minister. He compiled the Chaturvarga Chintamani, a vast digest of law, vows and gifts, and led their armies. Tradition credits him with the Modi script and with the Hemadpanti temples of dark stone fitted without mortar; historians doubt he invented either. Mahadeva, meanwhile, was beaten by the Kakatiya queen Rudrama.",
      "today": "Hemadpanti temples stand across Maharashtra, such as Gondeshwar at Sinnar."
    },
    {
      "place": "nevasa",
      "phase": "kingdom",
      "title": "Dnyaneshwar at Nevasa",
      "native": "ज्ञानेश्वरी",
      "when": "1290 CE",
      "at": 74,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          75,
          19.7
        ],
        "width": 9
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "In 1290, at Nevasa on the Pravara, the young Dnyaneshwar gave a commentary on the Bhagavad Gita in Marathi verse, the Dnyaneshwari, for ordinary people to hear. Its closing lines name the town and King Ramachandra of the Yadava line. Tradition says he was about fifteen, and that he took samadhi at Alandi in 1296. Warkari pilgrims still sing him.",
      "today": "Nevasa, Ahilyanagar district; a temple there keeps the pillar he is said to have leaned on."
    },
    {
      "place": "devagiri",
      "phase": "delhi",
      "title": "Gold for Alauddin",
      "native": "देवगिरी",
      "when": "1296 CE",
      "at": 80,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          78.3,
          22.8
        ],
        "width": 16
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "kara",
          "to": "devagiri",
          "traveller": "delhi-army",
          "style": "delhi",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "In 1296 Alauddin Khalji, governor of Kara, rode south with, by one count, 8,000 horsemen, posing as a noble leaving Delhi’s service. Ramachandra’s army was away with his son. Taken by surprise, the king held out in the citadel until he agreed to a vast ransom in gold, silver and pearls. Some sources date the raid to 1294.",
      "today": "The rock-cut moat and the dark passage to the citadel at Daulatabad."
    },
    {
      "place": "devagiri",
      "phase": "delhi",
      "title": "Malik Kafur takes Devagiri",
      "native": "देवगिरी",
      "when": "1307 to 1308 CE",
      "at": 86,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.2,
          24.3
        ],
        "width": 17
      },
      "legs": [
        {
          "from": "delhi",
          "to": "devagiri",
          "traveller": "kafur-army",
          "style": "delhi",
          "bend": 0.1
        }
      ],
      "text": "When the tribute stopped, Alauddin sent his general Malik Kafur. In 1307 Kafur’s army took Devagiri, and Ramachandra was sent to Delhi. Alauddin received him with honour, gave him the title ‘king of kings’ (rai rayan) and sent him home as a vassal. Devagiri became Delhi’s base for the south, and Ramachandra supplied Kafur’s marches on Warangal and Dorasamudra.",
      "today": "Daulatabad Fort is protected by the Archaeological Survey of India."
    },
    {
      "place": "devagiri",
      "phase": "delhi",
      "title": "The end of the Yadavas",
      "when": "1311 to 1318 CE",
      "at": 92,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.4,
          19.7
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "Ramachandra died about 1311, and his son Singhana III, or Shankaradeva, stopped the tribute. Kafur returned in 1313, killed him and governed the Deccan from Devagiri. After Alauddin died in 1316, Ramachandra’s son-in-law Harapaladeva rose in revolt, but in 1317 to 1318 Sultan Mubarak Shah crushed it and had him flayed alive. The kingdom became a province of Delhi.",
      "today": "Devagiri’s hill still rises above the plain north-west of Chhatrapati Sambhajinagar."
    },
    {
      "place": "devagiri",
      "phase": "after",
      "title": "Daulatabad",
      "native": "दौलताबाद",
      "when": "1327 to 1347 CE",
      "at": 98,
      "camera": {
        "at": [
          76.4,
          19.7
        ],
        "width": 10
      },
      "legs": [],
      "text": "In 1327 Muhammad bin Tughlaq renamed Devagiri as Daulatabad, ‘city of fortune’, made it a second capital and ordered much of Delhi’s elite to move there; most later returned. In 1347 nobles of the Deccan rebelled at Daulatabad and founded the Bahmani Sultanate, which ruled from Gulbarga. A Bahmani victory tower, the Chand Minar, still stands below the fort.",
      "today": "Daulatabad Fort and the Chand Minar, Maharashtra."
    }
  ],
