  "people": {
    "heading": "Kings, a minister and a saint",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "singhana-ii",
        "name": "Singhana II",
        "native": "सिंघण",
        "life": "r. 1210 to 1246",
        "role": "The greatest Yadava king",
        "steps": [
          2
        ],
        "text": "He fought on every side: against the Hoysalas in the south, the Kakatiyas in the east, the Paramaras of Malwa and the Vaghelas of Gujarat. Under him the kingdom reached its widest, from the Narmada valley to beyond the Krishna, though its borders shifted often. His court drew poets, astronomers and the musician Sharngadeva."
      },
      {
        "id": "hemadri",
        "name": "Hemadri",
        "native": "हेमाद्री",
        "life": "active c. 1260 to 1300",
        "role": "Chief minister and scholar",
        "steps": [
          4
        ],
        "text": "A Brahmin scholar who rose to run the treasury and the state under Mahadeva and Ramachandra. Besides the Chaturvarga Chintamani he wrote on medicine, a commentary on Vagbhata’s Ashtanga Hridaya. Many Maharashtra temples are called Hemadpanti after him, though many were built before or after his time, and his invention of the Modi script is a tradition."
      },
      {
        "id": "dnyaneshwar",
        "name": "Dnyaneshwar",
        "native": "ज्ञानेश्वर",
        "life": "c. 1275 to 1296, by tradition",
        "role": "Saint-poet",
        "steps": [
          5
        ],
        "text": "Tradition says his father had become a monk and then returned to family life, so the children were shunned at Alandi. Besides the Dnyaneshwari he wrote the Amritanubhav, on devotion and oneness. With Namdev he is honoured as a founder of the Warkari tradition, whose pilgrims carry his sandals (paduka) in a palanquin from Alandi to Pandharpur each year."
      },
      {
        "id": "ramachandra",
        "name": "Ramachandra",
        "native": "रामचंद्र",
        "life": "r. 1271 to c. 1311",
        "role": "The last independent Yadava king",
        "steps": [
          5,
          6,
          7
        ],
        "text": "He seized the throne from his cousin Amana in 1271 and ruled for forty years. He lost Devagiri’s gold to Alauddin in 1296 and his freedom to Malik Kafur in 1307, but chose loyalty afterwards, and Delhi rewarded him. His son and son-in-law rebelled after his death, and the kingdom did not survive them."
      }
    ]
